.TH URLENCODETEST 1
.SH NAME
urlencodetest \- regression test for urlencode(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B urlencodetest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Urlencodetest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/urlencode.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(argv,\ input) ,
and checks it against a real, live oracle - but unlike every other
oracle in this backlog, that oracle does not already exist anywhere on
this host: no plan9port copy of urlencode(1) exists, and no prebuilt
binary for it either.
.PP
Instead,
.I urlencodetest
BUILDS the real oracle itself, every run, by invoking
.B clang
(via
.IR cmd (3),
the same
.B #C
host-command-execution device every other oracle in this backlog
uses) directly against the ACTUAL 9front source
.RB ( /Users/npe/src/plan9front/sys/src/cmd/urlencode.c )
and plan9port's own prebuilt
.BR libc / libbio
(x86_64-only on this host, hence the explicit
.B "-arch x86_64"
in the build command). If the build ever fails - a moved checkout, a
missing compiler - this test fails loudly with the compiler's own
error text rather than silently skipping the comparison.
.PP
A real finding from building this test, not previously seen anywhere
else in this backlog: the built oracle binary lives entirely in the
HOST's own filesystem namespace (see
.IR cmd (3)'s
own note that a host command "runs in the host operating system's own
file name space", not Inferno's) - so
.BR sys->stat() / sys->remove()
(which only ever see INFERNO's own namespace) can never see it, and an
early version of this test's own success check using
.B sys->stat()
always reported failure even when the real host build had genuinely
succeeded. The
.B #C
.B wait
file's own exit-status field (its last, quoted field - empty for
success, confirmed directly by probing both a clean and a failing host
command) is the only reliable signal available from Inferno's own side
for a command whose output lives purely on the host.
.PP
Piping
.I input
into the built oracle's stdin uses the same
.B "winfd = nil"
pattern
.IR dc.b 's
own test suite established (see the
.B inferno-rio-cmd-device-oracle
memory) - dropping the last reference to the write-side
.B data
file forces an immediate close, delivering real end-of-file to the
oracle's stdin.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/urlencodetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR urlencode (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not exercise the
.I file
command-line-argument path (every case here drives
.BR run() 's
own
.I input
byte array directly, matching
.BR urlencode.b 's
own test-seam scope - see its man page).
